---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";
import { board6 } from "../lib/board6";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
const paperPdf = `${base}downloads/paper.pdf`;
const leanTarball = `${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`;

const anatomy1 = board6({
  labels: [
    [2, 2, "c"],
    [1, 2, "a"],
    [3, 2, "a"],
    [2, 1, "a"],
    [2, 3, "a"],
    [1, 1, "d"],
    [1, 3, "d"],
    [3, 1, "d"],
    [3, 3, "d"],
  ],
  squares: [[1, 1, 1]],
});
const anatomy2 = board6({
  labels: [
    [3, 2, "c"],
    [1, 2, "a"],
    [5, 2, "a"],
    [3, 0, "a"],
    [3, 4, "a"],
    [1, 0, "d"],
    [1, 4, "d"],
    [5, 0, "d"],
    [5, 4, "d"],
  ],
  squares: [[1, 0, 2]],
});
const basic = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [2, 1],
    [1, 2],
    [2, 3],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [4, 4],
    [3, 1],
    [1, 1],
  ],
  threats: [
    [1, 3],
    [3, 3],
  ],
});
const longest = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [3, 3],
    [4, 3],
    [3, 4],
    [1, 2],
    [5, 2],
    [3, 0],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [3, 1],
    [2, 3],
    [4, 2],
    [4, 4],
    [1, 4],
    [5, 4],
  ],
  threats: [
    [1, 0],
    [5, 0],
  ],
});
const branchPoint = board6({
  size: 5,
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
  ],
  white: [
    [4, 4],
    [2, 3],
  ],
  quiet: [[0, 2]],
});
---

<Base
  title="How the proof works · The Square Achievement Game"
  description="An illustrated guide to the proof that the first player wins the square achievement game exactly when n is at least 5: the plus rule, the hidden corner, the small boards and the 5 by 5 table, the forced checker, the simulation theorem for every larger board, and the Lean formalization."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>How the proof works</h1>
    <p class="byline">An illustrated guide to the square achievement game</p>
  </header>

  <p class="abstract">
    <strong>Summary.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four
    corners of an axis-parallel square wins, and a full board without one is a
    draw. The first player has a winning strategy if and only if
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />. For <span set:html={k("n \\ge 6")} /> one
    strategy serves every board: a rule with no lookup table whose moves after
    the second reply are all forcing, built around the plus, a stone with the
    four cells at a common distance from it along the axes, a shape proposed for
    this game by Sirgedas in 2010. The rule is checked on the boards from 6 × 6
    to 17 × 17, a simulation theorem carries one checked run on the 23 × 23
    board to every larger board, and the proofs are formalized in Lean 4. This
    guide follows the paper section by section, with the proofs left out.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="6"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 1. The bot follows the rule described below and explains each move.
      Change n to explore.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Introduction</h2>
  <p>
    Two players alternately claim empty cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, the first player moving first. A
    player wins upon holding all four corners of a square with horizontal and
    vertical sides, of any size: the cells
    <span
      set:html={k("(r,c),\\,(r,c{+}s),\\,(r{+}s,c),\\,(r{+}s,c{+}s)")}
    />{" "}
    for some <span set:html={k("s \\ge 1")} />. If the board fills with no
    player holding such a square, the game is a draw. The case
    <span set:html={k("s = 1")} /> is allowed, so the smallest winning shape is
    a 2 × 2 block; tilted squares do not count.
  </p>
  <p>
    Erickson posed the game and asked for the outcome under optimal play{" "}
    <a href="#ref-2">[2]</a>, and the question is recorded on the Open Problem
    Garden <a href="#ref-3">[3]</a> and on MathOverflow{" "}
    <a href="#ref-12">[12]</a>. It is an achievement game in the sense of Harary{" "}
    <a href="#ref-1">[1]</a>: both players pursue the same goal, and a player
    who loses the race may still secure a draw. The convention goes back to the
    positional games of Hales and Jewett <a href="#ref-8">[8]</a>; a recent
    unified treatment of achievement conventions is by Galliot and Sénizergues{" "}
    <a href="#ref-10">[10]</a>, and the general theory of positional games is in
    Beck <a href="#ref-11">[11]</a>. Two boundary results were known. Jenrich
    reported computer searches showing that the second player can secure a draw
    on 3 × 3 and 4 × 4 and that the first player can force a win on 5 × 5{" "}
    <a href="#ref-4">[4]</a>. Bacher and Eliahou proved that every two-coloring
    of the 15 × 15 grid contains four equal-colored cells at the corners of an
    axis-parallel square, and that 15 is least with this property{" "}
    <a href="#ref-5">[5]</a>; since no completed game on a board of size 15 or
    more is square-free, strategy stealing and determinacy give the first player
    a win there, though they produce no strategy. Between these results nothing
    was published, and no coloring argument can reach the boards from 6 × 6 to
    14 × 14, since Bacher and Eliahou also exhibit square-free two-colorings of
    the 14 × 14 board. One idea had been sketched: answering Erickson's question
    on MathOverflow in 2010, Sirgedas proposed that the first player open in the
    centre and win by making a <em>cross</em>, a stone with its four neighbours
    at a common distance along the axes, each arm forcing a reply{" "}
    <a href="#ref-13">[13]</a>; he noted that the cross can also be built on two
    stones in a row or column at distance two, worked out three of the nine
    first replies on the 7 × 7 board up to symmetry, and guessed that 5 × 5 was
    a draw. Concurrently with this work, Adler announced computer searches
    settling those nine sizes as first-player wins, with full strategy trees
    certified for 6 × 6 and 7 × 7 and search verdicts for the rest,
    cross-checked by independent runs at the smaller sizes{" "}
    <a href="#ref-9">[9]</a>. What is new here is a single strategy that wins
    every board from 6 × 6 up, stated in a paragraph, and a proof checked by a
    proof assistant. The bot in Figure 1 plays that strategy.
  </p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <span class="label">Theorem.</span> In the square achievement game on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board, the first player has a winning
    strategy if and only if <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />. For
    <span set:html={k("n \\le 4")} /> the game is drawn under optimal play: each
    player can secure at least a draw. For every
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 6")} />, the first player following the plus rule
    wins.
  </div>

  <p>
    The rule is built around one shape, the plus, which is Sirgedas's cross: the
    arms of a plus force the second player's replies one at a time, and the last
    arm threatens two squares at once. The sketch of 2010 covered one board and
    a few replies and did not say which plus to aim for, how to prepare one when
    the replies obstruct it, or why one board's analysis should carry to
    another; the rule settles the first two by a search with a fixed preference
    order, and the simulation theorem settles the third. The theorem for the
    rule is proved by checking the rule's forced tree on every board from 6 to
    17 and by a simulation theorem, stated for any strategy, that carries a
    checked run on the 23 × 23 board to every larger board. The outcome theorem
    follows with an exhaustive refutation for
    <span set:html={k("n \\le 4")} /> and a strategy table for
    <span set:html={k("n = 5")} />, whose play from the opening (2,2) is not
    purely forcing. Everything is formalized in Lean 4 <a href="#ref-6">[6]</a>{" "}
    on mathlib <a href="#ref-7">[7]</a>, and the formal theorem for the rule
    states that the tree of the rule's own moves is winning, not only that a
    winning tree exists. The rule was extracted from an earlier lookup-table
    strategy, almost all of whose forcing plans were arms of a side-1 plus at
    (2,2) or (3,2), the rest preparing the side-2 plus at (3,2).
  </p>

  <h2>Definitions</h2>
  <p>
    Cells are pairs <span set:html={k("(r,c)")} />, row then column, with row 0
    at the top and column 0 at the left in the figures. The first player
    <em>threatens</em> at an empty cell if that cell together with three of the
    first player's stones is a square; the second player <em>blocks</em> by
    playing it. A move that makes exactly one threat is <em>forcing</em>; a move
    that makes two threats wins, since one block leaves the other. One move of
    either player is a <em>ply</em>, so the first player moves at the odd plies.
    The rule plays inside the <em>region</em>, the 6 × 6 corner of the board,
    and reads the second player's stones inside the <em>base board</em>, the 17
    × 17 corner.
  </p>

  <h3>Pluses</h3>
  <p>
    A <em>plus</em> of side <span set:html={k("s")} /> at a <em>centre</em> cell
    has four <em>arms</em>, the cells at distance <span set:html={k("s")} />
    above, below, left, and right of the centre, and four <em>diagonals</em>,
    the cells at distance <span set:html={k("s")} /> in both directions. Two
    arms are <em>perpendicular</em> if one is above or below the centre and the
    other to its left or right; the diagonal between them is the cell in the row
    of one and the column of the other. The centre, two perpendicular arms, and
    the diagonal between them are the four corners of a square of side
    <span set:html={k("s")} />. The region holds sixteen pluses of side 1 and
    four of side 2; side 3 does not fit.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 316 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Two 6 by 6 grids: the plus of side 1 at (2,2) and the plus of side 2 at (3,2), with centre, arms, and diagonals labelled"
    >
      <defs>
        <pattern
          id="grid6p"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
        >
          <path
            d="M24 0H0V24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--rule)"
            stroke-width="0.8"
            opacity="0.5"
          ></path>
        </pattern>
      </defs>
      <g set:html={anatomy1} />
      <g transform="translate(164 0)" set:html={anatomy2} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 2. Left, the plus of side 1 at (2,2); right, the plus of side 2 at
      (3,2). The centre is c, the arms a, the diagonals d. The dashed outline is
      one of the four squares of each plus.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Suppose the first player holds the centre and one arm of a plus, and the
    second player holds none of its arms and none of its diagonals. Playing an
    arm perpendicular to the held arm threatens at the diagonal between them, so
    the second player must block there or lose. Once the first player also holds
    the arm opposite the held one, the remaining arm is perpendicular to both
    and threatens at two diagonals at once, which no single block answers. So
    from a centre and one arm the first player reaches a double threat in three
    moves against a second player who has no threat and whose blocks make none.
    The opening (2,2) and the second move (3,2) are the centre and an arm of the
    side-1 plus at (2,2), and an arm and the centre of the side-1 plus at (3,2).
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="The end of the basic line: black holds the plus of side 1 at (2,2) except the last arm's two threat cells"
    >
      <g set:html={basic} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 3. The basic line against the replies (0,0) and (4,4). The first
      player (black) holds (2,2) and (3,2); the arms (2,1) and (1,2) each
      threatened at a diagonal and the second player (white) blocked; the last
      arm (2,3) threatens at both dashed cells, and the win comes at ply 11.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Far stones and the hidden corner</h3>
  <p>
    The rule's stones never leave the region, and the second player's stones in
    a position the rule reaches are two free replies and forced blocks, the
    blocks in the region as well. Two distinct corners of a square that both lie
    in the region confine the square to the 12 × 12 corner of the board. Hence a
    second-player stone beyond the base board shares no square with two blocks,
    and a second-player square through such a stone and a block has the two free
    replies as its other corners. That block is the one cell through which
    stones beyond the base board can affect the game.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let <span set:html={k("x")} /> and <span set:html={k("y")} /> be two cells
    each having a coordinate at least 12. Their <em>hidden corner</em> is a cell
    of the region that together with <span set:html={k("x")} /> and
    <span set:html={k("y")} /> makes three corners of one square. Two cells have
    at most one hidden corner. The <em>abstraction</em> of a position consists
    of the first player's stones, the second player's stones inside the base
    board, and, once the second player has two stones, the hidden corner of the
    first two, if they have one. A second player forced to block at the hidden
    corner would hold three corners of that square and threaten at its fourth, a
    cell the rule does not read. So the rule never makes a threat whose only
    block is the hidden corner. It applies the restriction whenever the pair has
    a hidden corner, even when the square through it lies inside the base board
    and the restriction is not needed. The threshold 12 and the base 17 are tied
    by 17 = 12 + 5: a square through a region cell leaves the base board only if
    its side is at least 12. With a threshold of 11 and base 16 the argument
    would still work, and 11 is the least threshold for which a pair has at most
    one hidden corner; the rule has been checked only with base 17.
  </p>

  <h3>Tempo moves and lines</h3>
  <p>
    When no plus can be completed directly, because the second player holds an
    arm or a diagonal or because the arms would not force, the rule first plays
    moves that each force a block and so add a stone. In a position where the
    second player has no threat, a <em>tempo move</em> is a cell in the region
    that makes exactly one threat, such that the threat cell is not the hidden
    corner and after the block the second player still has no threat. A
    <em>line</em> from a position consists of some tempo moves, each followed by
    its block, and then the arms the first player does not yet hold of one plus
    in the region whose centre the first player holds, in some order, such that
    each arm before the last makes exactly one threat whose block is not the
    hidden corner, the last arm makes at least two threats, and the second
    player never has a threat. The <em>key</em> of a line is (number of tempo
    moves, number of arms to play, side of the plus, centre of the plus). The
    rule prefers the smallest key in lexicographic order, cells ordered by row
    and then by column, then the line its search generates first. It searches
    for lines with at most three tempo moves. Whether a line exists, and which
    is preferred, depends only on the abstraction of the position.
  </p>

  <h2>The plus rule</h2>
  <p>
    The move function has five clauses, tried in order. If some empty cell
    completes a square of the first player, play the least such cell. Otherwise,
    if some empty cell completes a square of the second player, play the least
    such cell. Otherwise, if the first player has no stone, play (2,2).
    Otherwise, if the first player does not yet hold the second move, play it:
    (3,2) against every first reply except seven cells of the region, for which
    the paper gives the move in a small diagram. Otherwise play the first cell
    the first player does not hold of the line chosen, once, from the
    abstraction after the second reply. The seven exceptions are exactly the
    first replies from which (3,2) fails; they were found by search. If the
    search returns no line the rule is undefined, and the checker of the
    verification section certifies that this never happens at a position the
    rule reaches.
  </p>
  <p>
    The paper lists two complete games against a second player who always
    blocks. In the first the replies (0,0) and (4,4) are away from the opening
    pair and the basic line of Figure 3 wins at once, key (0, 3, 1, (2,2)). In
    the second the reply (3,1) is a diagonal of both side-1 pluses, no line with
    fewer than two tempo moves exists, and the line has key (2, 4, 2, (3,2)):
    two tempo moves and then the four arms of the side-2 plus at (3,2). Figure 4
    shows the position before its last move.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="The end of the longest line: two tempo moves prepared the plus of side 2 at (3,2)"
    >
      <g set:html={longest} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 4. The longest line, against the replies (0,0) and (3,1), after ply
      15. The tempo moves (3,3) and (4,3) were each blocked; then the arms
      (3,4), (1,2), (5,2) of the side-2 plus at (3,2) each forced a block, two
      of them on the diagonals (1,4) and (5,4), and the last arm (3,0) threatens
      at the other two diagonals, dashed.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Since the blocks are forced, every position of the forced tree below the
    second reply is determined by the line, and the first player's stones at any
    point are the opening pair and a prefix of it. Committing to the line makes
    the forced tree a function of the reply pair, which the argument for boards
    of size at least 17 uses.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Python implementation of the rule was run over every reply pair on each
    board from 6 to 17. Every board plays exactly the 79 lines of the region,
    since a pair's line depends only on its abstraction. On the 6 × 6 board, 985
    reply pairs need no tempo move, 91 need one, 54 need two, and 25 need three;
    the 79 pairs needing two or three tempo moves have both replies in the
    region and are the same on every board, while the other two counts grow with
    the board. Every pair won by a side-1 plus needs no tempo move and every
    pair won by a side-2 plus needs at least one. The longest line, the second
    game above, has six first-player moves after the opening pair, and every
    game in which the second player always blocks ends by ply 17; a reply that
    blocks nothing leaves a threat that the rule completes at its next move. So
    the rule wins every game by the seventeenth ply. This bound is computed from
    the 79 lines and is not part of the formal theorem.
  </p>

  <h2>Small boards and the 5 × 5 board</h2>
  <p>
    The second player has no winning strategy on any board, by strategy
    stealing. Had the second player a winning strategy, the first player could
    open with an arbitrary move and thereafter follow that strategy as if the
    extra stone were absent, making a further arbitrary move whenever it selects
    an occupied cell. Surplus stones never hurt, so the first player would win,
    against the assumption that the strategy wins for the second player. For
    <span set:html={k("n \\le 4")} /> the first player has no winning strategy
    either. For <span set:html={k("n \\le 2")} /> the board has at most four
    cells, so neither player ever holds four. For
    <span set:html={k("n = 3, 4")} /> an exhaustive search that is part of the
    formal development exhibits, for every first-player move, a reply after
    which the first player still cannot force a win. The step from neither
    player forcing a win to each player securing a draw is determinacy of the
    finite game, proved by induction on the number of empty cells. So the boards
    up to 4 × 4 are drawn under optimal play.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 5 × 5 board is won by a table, extracted from a full solution of the 5 ×
    5 game (<span set:html={k("1.79 \\times 10^8")} /> positions solved, with
    draws scored against the first player) and verified by a checker that walks
    the complete game tree, every reply at every node. Like the rule, it
    completes a square when it can and otherwise blocks one before consulting
    the table. It opens at (2,2), gives a second move for each of the 24 first
    replies, and after the second reply follows a forcing plan where one exists.
    For 104 of the reply pairs, with the table's second move, the solver finds
    no winning continuation of at most eight moves in which every first-player
    move makes a threat, so the table plays a <em>quiet</em> move there, one
    making no threat, and continues with a sub-table indexed by the reply. Such
    a position is a <em>branch point</em> (Figure 5). That no such continuation
    exists is a computation of the solver and is not formalized; the win of the
    table is. The table plays in Figure 1 when n is set to 5.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 128 128"
      role="img"
      aria-label="A 5 by 5 board: black holds (2,2) and (3,2), white holds (4,4) and (2,3), and a hollow square marks the quiet move (0,2)"
    >
      <g set:html={branchPoint} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 5. A branch point of the 5 × 5 table. The first player (black)
      opened at (2,2) and answered the reply (4,4) with the table's second move
      (3,2). After the second reply (2,3), one of the 104 pairs with no forcing
      continuation, the table plays the quiet move (0,2), marked by the square,
      and then consults a sub-table indexed by the reply to it.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Verification</h2>
  <p>
    Fix a strategy and a board size. The <em>full tree</em> of the strategy is
    the game tree in which the first player's moves are given by the strategy
    and the second player's moves range over all empty cells; the strategy
    verifies if every branch ends in a first-player square, and then the first
    player wins. The 5 × 5 table is checked on its full tree.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rule is checked on a smaller tree. The <em>forced tree</em> has the
    first player playing the strategy's move, the second player's replies
    ranging over all empty cells at the first two levels, and below every later
    node only over the cells that block a threat of the move just played. The
    checker walks this tree under four guards. (G1) After the second reply,
    every move of the strategy wins outright or makes a threat. (G2) Every
    non-winning move and every threat cell lies in the region. (G3) After every
    move of the strategy the second player has no threat. (G4) Optionally, for a
    given cell, no move of the strategy has that cell as its only threat cell. A
    reply that blocks nothing leaves a threat standing, which the strategy
    completes at its next move, so every omitted reply loses in two plies. The
    soundness theorem of the forced checker says that if a strategy plays a
    completion whenever one exists and its forced tree passes the guards with
    every branch ending in a first-player square, then the tree in which the
    first player plays the strategy's move at every node is winning.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rule passes the forced tree on every board from 6 to 17, one run of the
    checker per board, with
    <span set:html={k("(n^2-1)(n^2-3)")} /> reply pairs at the root, from 1,155
    pairs at 6 to 82,368 at 17; the runs take from under a second to 72 seconds
    each. At every node the checker evaluates the rule afresh; no table is
    consulted. A Python implementation of the rule and a full-tree verifier,
    used while the rule was being found, agree with the Lean definition on the
    line committed to for every reply pair of the 6 × 6 board and for the
    anchored positions of the next section, and the full-tree verifier confirms
    the win on 6 × 6. Only the Lean runs enter the proof. The bot on this page
    is a TypeScript copy of the rule, and the site's tests replay it against the
    lines the Python implementation commits to on the same positions.
  </p>

  <h2>Boards of size at least 17</h2>
  <p>
    For every <span set:html={k("n \\ge 17")} /> the first player following the
    rule wins. A stone beyond the base board can affect the rule's play only
    through a hidden corner, and the rule never lets a block land on one. The
    proof replays a checked run on the 23 × 23 board as a run on the larger
    board. Replies inside the base board are read verbatim. A reply beyond the
    base board is replaced, for the base run, by a <em>stand-in</em>: a fixed
    cell beyond the base board when the pair has no hidden corner, and an
    <em>anchor</em>, a cell beyond the base board that forms with the other
    reply the same hidden corner, when it has one. The <em>anchor board</em>, 23
    × 23, is the least board holding every anchor. The anchor board, the
    stand-ins, and the anchors are choices made in the proof, and the rule does
    not depend on them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The simulation theorem is stated for any strategy. Take a real history of
    the opening pair and two replies on a board of size at least 17, and a base
    history on another board of size at least 17 with the same first-player
    moves, in which each reply is either the same cell inside the base board or,
    for a reply beyond the base board, some cell beyond the 12 × 12 corner.
    Suppose that (i) on every history extending the real one in which the first
    player's stones lie in the region, the strategy plays a cell completing one
    of the first player's squares whenever one exists; (ii) the strategy plays
    the same move after the real history and after the base history, whatever
    further moves follow both; (iii) the forced tree of the strategy below the
    base history passes the guards, with a chosen cell as the cell of (G4) if
    there is one, and every branch ends in a first-player square; and (iv) if a
    reply lies beyond the base board, then either no cell was chosen and the
    reply pair has no hidden corner, or the chosen cell is the pair's hidden
    corner. Then the tree in which the first player plays the strategy's move at
    every node below the real history is winning. For the rule, hypotheses (i)
    and (ii) are lemmas about its definition. The rule's move depends on a
    history only through its abstraction and through the second move of the
    first reply; a reply beyond the base board and its stand-in both drop out of
    the abstraction, the stand-in pair is chosen with the same hidden corner as
    the real pair, and every first reply beyond the region, and every stand-in,
    has the default second move (3,2), so the two abstractions agree at every
    node.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pairs with a hidden corner <span set:html={k("b = (b_1, b_2)")} /> are
    covered by three families of <em>anchored runs</em>, each a forced run of
    the rule on the anchor board from the opening pair and an anchor pair, with
    <span set:html={k("b")} /> as the cell of (G4), preceded by a check that
    <span set:html={k("b")} /> is the hidden corner of the anchor pair. The
    <em>far</em> family, for both replies beyond the base board, uses the
    anchors <span set:html={k("(b_1 + 17,\\, b_2 + 17)")} /> and
    <span set:html={k("(b_1 + 17,\\, b_2)")} />, two corners of the square of
    side 17 anchored at <span set:html={k("b")} />; one run per cell of the
    region, 36 runs. The <em>exact</em> family, for a first reply inside the
    base board and a second reply beyond it, keeps the first reply, which lies
    in the row or the column of <span set:html={k("b")} /> at distance at least
    12, and places the second anchor at the corner opposite
    <span set:html={k("b")} /> of the square through
    <span set:html={k("b")} /> and the first reply; 70 runs. The
    <em>far-near</em> family, for a first reply beyond the base board and a
    second reply inside it, is the exact family with the two replies exchanged;
    70 runs. This gives 176 anchored runs in all. The theorem for boards of size
    at least 17 dispatches five cases to the simulation theorem: neither reply
    beyond the base board, which is a branch of the forced run on the anchor
    board; a reply beyond it and no hidden corner, with the stand-ins (22,22)
    and (22,21), again a branch of that run; and the three cases with a hidden
    corner, one per anchored family.
  </p>

  <h2>Formalization</h2>
  <p>
    The Lean development defines cells, boards, and squares as above, and the
    winning predicate <code>P1CanWin</code> as an inductive family whose
    derivations are exactly strategy trees: one first-player move per node,
    every reply covered, draws failures by construction. The second player's win
    is defined from it by swapping roles after a move; the first player's
    ability to secure a draw is a second inductive family, whose branches may
    also end on a full board, and the second player's is defined from that by
    the same swap. Strategy stealing and determinacy are proved against these
    definitions with no computation. These definitions, a few dozen lines, are
    the whole specification; everything else is proved against them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The predicate behind the theorem for the rule is <code>WinsWith</code>. For
    a strategy <code>σ</code>, a board size <code>n</code>, and a history
    <code>h</code> with the first player to move, <code>WinsWith σ n h</code>
    holds if the move of <code>σ</code> at <code>h</code> is legal and either
    completes a square or admits at least one reply, no reply completes a
    second-player square, and <code>WinsWith</code> holds again after every
    reply. It implies
    <code>P1CanWin</code> by forgetting that the move is the one
    <code>σ</code> returns. The theorem for the rule is
  </p>
  <pre class="lean"><code>theorem plus_wins_with {"{"}n : ℕ{"}"} (h : 6 ≤ n) :
    WinsWith plusMove n []</code></pre>
  <p>
    where <code>plusMove</code> is the rule as a Lean definition, 250 lines with
    no data beyond the seven exceptional second moves. Three executable checkers
    are proved sound: the table checker, the forced checker, which is generic
    over strategies, and the refuter for the small boards. Each soundness proof
    is an induction over the checker's recursion, so it never inspects the
    strategy. Hypotheses (i) and (ii) of the simulation theorem for the rule are
    <code>plusMove_takesWins</code> and <code>plusMove_agree</code>, proved by
    unfolding the definition; the simulation theorem is <code>simulate</code>,
    and the outcome theorem is <code>outcome</code>. Concrete boards are
    discharged by native evaluation (<code>native_decide</code>): the
    refutations at 1 to 4, the 5 × 5 run, one evaluation for the rule's runs at
    sizes 6 to 16, one for 17, one for the anchor board, and one per anchored
    family. The axioms of <code>outcome</code> and <code>plus_wins_with</code>
    are therefore <code>propext</code>, <code>Classical.choice</code>,
    <code>Quot.sound</code>, and one instance of Lean's evaluation axiom per
    certificate; the kernel checks the soundness theorems but not the
    evaluations, which the Lean compiler and runtime perform. The run on the
    anchor board, 277,728 reply pairs, takes 499 seconds, the 176 anchored runs
    take 0.34 seconds together, and the whole development builds in about 17
    minutes. Both artifacts are built from this repository on every deploy:
    <a href={paperPdf}>the paper (PDF)</a> and
    <a href={leanTarball}>the Lean development (tar.gz)</a>. The paper is on
    arXiv as <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22410">arXiv:2610.22410</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Open questions</h2>
  <p>
    The rule wins by ply 17, a bound computed from its lines. What is the least
    ply by which the first player can force a win on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board, and is there a rule of
    comparable length that attains it on every board? The second move has seven
    exceptions; is there an opening, or an opening with a fixed second move,
    from which a rule of this kind wins with none? Whether a short rule exists
    for 5 × 5, where the table needs quiet moves at branch points, is open. Base
    16 would serve the hidden-corner argument, but whether the rule still wins
    when it reads only the 16 × 16 board was not checked; whether a rule can
    read less than the second player's stones inside its base board, for
    instance only those inside the region with a summary of the rest, is open.
  </p>

